SEND and Inclusion
At St Joseph’s Catholic Primary School, we believe that every child is unique, valued and loved by God. We are committed to providing an inclusive education in which all children, including those with Special Educational Needs and Disabilities (SEND), are supported to participate fully in school life, make progress and achieve their potential.
We recognise that children’s needs are individual and can change over time. Our aim is to identify additional needs as early as possible and work closely with children, families, staff and outside professionals to put appropriate support in place.
For some pupils, this may involve adaptations to classroom teaching, additional resources or targeted interventions. For others, learning may need to be more significantly personalised and based on an individual child’s stage of development and particular needs.
Through high-quality teaching, appropriate adaptations, targeted support and personalised learning, we aim to help every child develop academically, socially and emotionally. We recognise and celebrate progress in all its forms, building on children’s strengths and helping them to develop confidence, independence and a positive sense of themselves as learners.
Our inclusive approach means that, wherever possible, children learn alongside their peers within the classroom. At times, carefully planned individual or small-group provision may also be used to develop particular skills or provide additional support.
Our staff work closely together to ensure that pupils with SEND are understood, supported and included throughout the school day. Partnership with parents and carers is central to our approach. We value open, respectful and honest communication and believe that the best outcomes for children are achieved when school and families work together.
Above all, we want every child at St Joseph’s to feel safe, welcomed, valued and able to flourish as part of our school community.
Examples of SEND Provision
The provision offered to pupils is based upon their individual needs. Support may include classroom adaptations, individual or small-group intervention and specialist programmes.
Zones of Regulation
At St Joseph’s, Zones of Regulation is used across the whole school with all pupils as part of our approach to supporting emotional awareness, self-regulation and wellbeing.
Children are taught to recognise and name how they are feeling and to understand that all emotions are valid. They learn to identify which “zone” best describes their emotional state and to develop a range of strategies that can help them manage their feelings and respond appropriately.
Using a shared language across the school helps children to:
recognise and communicate their emotions;
develop greater self-awareness;
understand how emotions can affect behaviour and learning;
build a toolkit of strategies to help them regulate;
develop resilience and independence; and
support positive relationships with others.
For some children with SEND, the Zones of Regulation approach may be further personalised or reinforced through additional individual or small-group support.
Attention Autism – “Bucket Time”
Attention Autism is an approach designed to develop attention, communication and shared interaction through highly engaging and motivating activities.
“Bucket Time” uses visually exciting and enjoyable objects and activities to capture children’s attention and encourage them to participate in a shared experience.
The approach can support children to:
develop attention and listening skills;
increase shared and joint attention;
enjoy participating in group activities;
develop spontaneous interaction;
strengthen non-verbal and verbal communication;
extend vocabulary and language; and
develop their ability to remain engaged in an activity for increasing periods of time.
Activities are carefully planned to be motivating, memorable and fun.
Sensory Circuits
Sensory Circuits are short, structured sessions designed to support children who benefit from additional sensory input and movement as part of their school day.
A sensory circuit may include a combination of activities that help children to alert, organise and calm their bodies, supporting them to become more regulated and ready for learning.
Depending on the needs of the child, Sensory Circuits can help to:
improve attention and concentration;
support sensory regulation;
develop balance, coordination and body awareness;
provide appropriate opportunities for movement;
support children to become calmer and more settled; and
help children prepare for learning and classroom routines.
Sensory Circuits are used where appropriate as part of a child’s individual provision and are adapted according to need.
ELSA – Emotional Literacy Support
ELSA stands for Emotional Literacy Support Assistant.
ELSA support is designed to help children develop their emotional wellbeing, recognising that children are better able to learn and thrive when their emotional needs are understood and supported.
Individual or small-group sessions may focus on areas such as:
recognising and managing emotions;
self-esteem and confidence;
friendships and social skills;
anxiety and worries;
loss and bereavement;
ELSA is a nationally recognised approach developed and supported by educational psychologists. Trained ELSAs receive ongoing professional supervision to support the quality and effectiveness of their work.
Further information about ELSA can be found through the ELSA Network.
Working with External Professionals
Where appropriate, St Joseph’s works in partnership with a range of external professionals and services to provide additional advice, assessment or support for children.
Havering SEND Services, including Educational Psychology and specialist advisory services;
Speech and Language Therapy;
a Wellbeing Practitioner, supporting children where there are identified emotional wellbeing needs;
services supporting children’s social, emotional and mental health needs;
health professionals and other specialist services;
Brentwood Catholic Children’s Society; and
other professionals involved in supporting an individual child or family.
Our work with external professionals may include consultation, advice for staff, direct work with children, assessment and recommendations for further support.
Parents and carers will be involved in discussions where additional specialist advice or support is being considered.
Contact Us
Our Inclusion Lead, Ms L. Wilson, oversees SEND provision across the school.
She can be contacted by email at:
senco@st-josephs.havering.sch.uk
Alternatively, parents and carers can contact the School Office to arrange an appointment.
We encourage families to speak to us if they have concerns about their child’s learning, development or wellbeing. Working together, we can ensure that children receive the right support at the right time.
